From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 02:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] target/i386/kvm: an incoming machine's TSC is written only
 from its migration

A machine made with -incoming writes each vCPU's TSC twice before the
migration loads: zero after the vCPU is made, then one at the machine's
reset (x86_cpu_reset_hold writes one instead of the TSC it read back, so
that KVM does not take the write for a hot-plugged vCPU's zero).  KVM
counts any TSC written by userspace as one userspace has set
(kvm->arch.user_set_tsc), and from then on takes a TSC written within a
second of what it expects as an attempt to keep the vCPUs in sync, and
keeps the offset it has.  The TSC the migration then writes is taken
that way whenever the saved guest had run for less than a second more
than this machine's vCPU has existed: the vCPU keeps counting from its
creation, and the guest's TSC goes back by its uptime at the save.

KVM's commit bf328e22e472 ("KVM: x86: Don't sync user-written TSC
against startup values", 6.7) is for exactly this restore, a guest that
has run for less than a second, and these writes undo it.  What it costs
a guest is its TSC-deadline timer.  The deadline comes back as saved and
is met only when the new count reaches it, the saved guest's uptime
after the vCPU's creation.  A guest whose tick was running at the save
had that deadline a tick ahead, and its hrtimers, all later than the
deadline it believes armed, are not programmed again: it sleeps until
the new count reaches the old deadline.  Traced in spin-machine (a
checkpoint taken 0.43 s into a boot, restored on nested KVM, kprobes on
kvm_synchronize_tsc), the TSC writes were 0, 1 and the saved
1324628232, the last matched to the first's offset; the guest answered
its first packets, then halted 281-450 ms with no timer interrupt and
no TSC-deadline write, and woke 530 ms after QEMU's exec however long
the load had taken.  That was 30% of restores, the ones whose guest was
saved with its tick running.

While a machine waits for an incoming migration, its TSC is written only
from migrated state: the full state put after the load, whose TSC is
never zero.  The first TSC KVM then gets from userspace is the
migration's, taken as given, and the vCPUs after it are kept in sync
with it.  A machine started any other way, and a reset once a machine
runs, write the TSC as before.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 target/i386/kvm/kvm.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/target/i386/kvm/kvm.c b/target/i386/kvm/kvm.c
index e29ecaba5..9255b683e 100644
--- a/target/i386/kvm/kvm.c
+++ b/target/i386/kvm/kvm.c
@@ -4250,7 +4250,18 @@ static int kvm_put_msrs(X86CPU *cpu, KvmPutState level)
      * for normal writeback. Limit them to reset or full state updates.
      */
     if (level >= KVM_PUT_RESET_STATE) {
-        kvm_msr_entry_add(cpu, MSR_IA32_TSC, env->tsc);
+        /*
+         * A machine waiting for an incoming migration has its TSC from
+         * the migration alone.  KVM takes the TSC userspace writes as
+         * given only if it is the first: written before the load, the
+         * creation's zero or the reset's one makes KVM take a migrated
+         * TSC within a second of it as an attempt to stay in sync, and
+         * keep the vCPU's TSC counting from its creation.
+         */
+        if (!runstate_check(RUN_STATE_INMIGRATE) ||
+            (level == KVM_PUT_FULL_STATE && env->tsc)) {
+            kvm_msr_entry_add(cpu, MSR_IA32_TSC, env->tsc);
+        }
         if (env->features[FEAT_KVM] & (CPUID_KVM_CLOCK | CPUID_KVM_CLOCK2)) {
             kvm_msr_entry_add(cpu, MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME, env->system_time_msr);
             kvm_msr_entry_add(cpu, MSR_KVM_WALL_CLOCK, env->wall_clock_msr);
-- 
